+- First run `bin/anylinuxfs shell --command 'true'` to initialize or update
+  Alpine. Copy `mtpfs/`, `LICENSE`, and `scripts/prepare-mtp-guest.sh` with
+  `scripts/libmtp-bounded-events.patch`, `scripts/libmtp-close-timeout.patch`,
+  `scripts/libmtp-response-status.patch`,
+  and `scripts/jmtpfs-fuse-open-handles.patch` into a directory in that rootfs,
+  preserving the repository layout. For example, use `/opt/anylinuxfs-source`.
+  Run `bin/anylinuxfs shell --rw-rootfs --command
+  'sh /opt/anylinuxfs-source/scripts/prepare-mtp-guest.sh'`.
+  This builds the first-party C++ helper and checksum-verified private libmtp
+  1.1.22 and libfuse 2.9.9 dependencies. The guest requires
+  `/usr/local/libexec/anylinuxfs/mtpfs`. Re-run preparation after source updates
+  or rootfs reinitialization. `build-app.sh` builds the host and VM components;
+  it does not build this optional guest helper.
+
+### MTP direct writes
+
+The helper keeps a session-local namespace, a sparse read cache, and one MTP
+transport owner. Each write waits for the phone to acknowledge its bytes;
+`fsync` and NFS COMMIT finish the object's MTP editing session. There is no
+background whole-file publisher, quiet-period snapshot, or automatic upload
+replay. A buffered client write alone is not a completed copy: applications
+must check their final synchronization result and unmount before disconnecting.
+
+FUSE uses cached write-through with big writes, not FUSE writeback caching or
+per-file direct I/O. USB requests are bounded to 1 MiB. NFS can deliver writes
+out of order; the helper extends the remote object before writing beyond EOF.
+Cached attributes do not wait for unrelated file I/O. Opening, renaming, or
+deleting a file does not download the whole file.
+
+Each host mount has a private loopback RPC endpoint. It routes mountd calls and
+limits NFSv3 to four outstanding writes and eight total requests across its
+connections. Pending payloads share a 16 MiB budget; connection count, framing
+buffers and request count are also bounded. A read burst quota allows waiting
+mutations to progress. Explicit file-handle barriers order related RPCs, while
+namespace consistency remains the filesystem's responsibility. This does not
+change the system-wide NFS write limit. The normal NFS timeout settings remain
+in effect; longer waits are not used as a throughput fix.
+
+A lost TCP connection after a dispatched mutation ends the session. The relay
+never recreates that backend connection or retries a possibly completed
+operation. The host checks the kernel mount table to distinguish Finder eject
+from an unexpected failure, unmounts a failed volume, and preserves diagnostics.
+Unmodified mount probes and read-only connections may close normally.
+
+Replacement rename retains the old target under a unique non-hidden
+`ALFS-retained-<uuid>` name before renaming the source. When native moves are
+available, retained objects move into a session-owned `ALFS-open-<uuid>` directory
+at the storage root, so they do not prevent removing the former user directory.
+Without native moves, a directory containing retained open objects remains
+nonempty until those handles close. Open handles continue using the old object
+until release. Cross-directory moves use a unique
+`ALFS-moving-<uuid>` intermediate name to avoid collisions. Namespace changes
+are published locally only after the MTP operations succeed. An uncertain
+partial operation stops the session and retains a small diagnostic record;
+there is no blind rollback or startup replay. MTP replacement is not atomic
+against phone-side applications or physical disconnects. A failed operation
+may leave a retained/intermediate object that needs manual inspection.
+
+Unlink keeps an open object's remote identity until the final handle closes.
+Cross-storage rename returns `EXDEV`; unsupported native moves return
+`EOPNOTSUPP`. Nonempty directory replacement/deletion is rejected. AppleDouble
+sidecars and `.DS_Store` are local to the mount; NFS-generated `.nfs` names are
+real remote objects. Moving between a local sidecar and a remote object is
+unsupported. Unix modes and timestamps are mount-local metadata: updates are
+visible in this mount, but remounting reloads the phone's timestamps. Samsung
+rejects DateModified updates with response A80A; the helper does not issue that
+unsupported property command or claim that these attributes persist on the phone.
+
+Use `anylinuxfs unmount mtp:BUS_ID` and wait for session shutdown before
+unplugging. After an interrupted upload, the phone can contain a partial file;
+reconnect, remount, and explicitly copy it again from the original source.
+`--mtp-recover` has been removed. Old caches are never opened, migrated, replayed,
+or automatically removed by the new helper. Preserve them for recovery with
+the version that created them if they contain pending user data.
+
+The session directory under `~/.anylinuxfs/mtp-cache/` contains plaintext read
+cache data and diagnostics, exported through private virtiofs. It is removed
+only after a clean session completes. It is not a complete source-file snapshot
+or a backup. Protocol acknowledgement is not a claim of physical power-loss
+durability on the phone. Keep file changes within this mount while it is active.
+
+The private libmtp response-status patch distinguishes complete protocol
+rejections from transport failures. Repeated handles in Samsung listings are
+deduplicated at the transport boundary; duplicate names remain an error. The
+bounded event reader and session-close fix for Harmony, and the private libfuse
+open-handle fix, remain in use. None replaces the system library.
